<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ezra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ezra was a Jewish priest and scribe who, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the Persian period with authority from the Achaemenid king Artaxerxes. He is credited with reading the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> to the people and enforcing it, including the dissolution of marriages with foreign wives. Jewish tradition regards him as a restorer of the Torah, and the biblical Book of Ezra bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezra.jpg" alt="Ezra" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Doré · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:109.Ezra_Reads_the_Law_to_the_People.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
